Performance improvements (identified via benchmarks in gisp_test.go)
======================================================================

The main bottleneck is function call overhead: every CallLambda invocation
allocates a new map[string]any via NewEnv (~128B + pointer overhead), giving
~6-7 heap allocations per call regardless of arity. fib(20) produces 54K
allocations as a result.

A. Slice-based frame for lambda environments  [high impact]
-----------------------------------------------------------
Replace the map[string]any in Env with a []any indexed by parameter
position for lambda call frames. Parameters are known and fixed at lambda
creation time, so a linear-scan slice is both faster and cheaper than a
hash map for the typical case of 1–4 params.

  type Frame struct {
      names []string  // shared from Lambda.args — no copy
      vals  []any
      next  *Env
  }

Expected: ~50% reduction in allocs/op for function-call-heavy programs.

B. Op/Cond as integer constants  [medium impact]
-------------------------------------------------
callop and callcond switch on strings ("+" , "-", "<=", ...).
Change Op.value and Cond.value from string to int8 (iota enum) so every
arithmetic/comparison dispatch is an integer switch instead of a string
comparison.

C. Intern common Integer values  [medium impact]
-------------------------------------------------
Values like 0, 1, -1 are created fresh on the heap on every arithmetic
result. Pre-allocate a table of boxed Integer singletons for the range
[-1, 255] (similar to Java's Integer.valueOf cache). Avoids one heap
alloc per arithmetic expression that yields a small constant result.

D. Symbol interning at parse time  [low-medium impact]
-------------------------------------------------------
Store a single canonical string per symbol name in a global table and use
pointer equality in lookupName instead of string comparison. Reduces hash
overhead in map lookups and enables a future slice-based env to use O(1)
indexed lookup keyed by symbol ID.

E. Tail-call optimization  [correctness / stack depth]
-------------------------------------------------------
There is no TCO; deep recursion uses Go stack frames linearly and will
overflow on large inputs. A trampoline or explicit stack in Eval would
allow tail-recursive programs (and iterative loops written as recursion)
to run without stack overflow.

F. String concatenation in the parser  [minor]
----------------------------------------------
The ident-scanning loop in parse() uses += on a string inside a loop,
which allocates on each iteration. Use a strings.Builder instead.
